with open('dashboard.html', 'w') as f:
    f.write(str(html))
```

This will create a simple HTML file that displays your data in a table. You can then use a web browser to view the HTML file.

To make the dashboard more interactive, you might want to use JavaScript to add buttons that allow the user to edit, insert, and remove data from the table. You can also use CSS to style the dashboard and make it more visually appealing.

Here's an example of how you might add some basic styling to the dashboard using CSS:

```css
table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
  width: 100%;
}

th, td {
  text-align: left;
  padding: 8px;
}

tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
}
```

This CSS will add some basic styling to the table, such as borders, padding, and alternating row colors.

To make the dashboard responsive, you can use CSS media queries to adjust the layout of the dashboard based on the screen size. Here's an example of how you might do this:

```css
@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {
  table {
    width: 100%;
  }

  th, td {
    display: block;
  }
}
```

This CSS will adjust the layout of the table when the screen size is less than 600 pixels wide, making it more suitable for mobile devices.

Finally, to add buttons for editing, inserting, and removing data, you can use JavaScript to add event listeners to the buttons and update the table accordingly. Here's an example of how you might do this:

